A||N||5||Bob Englert||Finding short||||1||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||12-16-2001||09:19 PM||||On my way home from our monthly Tarheel Fiero meeting, I noticed the voltage gauge was much lower than normal (about 9-10v).  The car got me home but when I went out to the car a few days later the battery was completely dead.  No even a hint at the dome light.<P>I haven't had any experience tracking down shorts and I was wondering if anyone has any suggestions on how to track this down. <P>If the alternator died during the trip, could it have discharged the battery to the point I found yet still got me home or would it be a real short to discharge the battery completely.<P>Any suggestions or links to older posts would be appreciated.  The car is an 87 GT that had a car alarm and car phone installed when I got it so the wiring harness, particularly around the fuse panel, is a little messed up.<P>Thanks...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-16-2001||09:27 PM||||If your alternator went, the distance you could go depends on how charged the battery was. You could have gotten home and the battery finally died.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTDude||12-16-2001||09:43 PM||||Yes, most likely an alternator problem.  Test it with a volt meter at idle.  Should be between 13.5 and 14.5 volts.  Maybe just above idle.  If this checks out ok....repost and I'll try to help.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||12-19-2001||10:38 PM||||You guys were right!  It appears that the alternator was the problem.  After spending way too much time getting the alternator out and the new one back in, the car is charging properly again.  When I had the old alternator bench tested at Autozone, it wasn't charging and the diode tested defective.<P>When I test drove the car this evening, the cruise control was working again.  I wonder if the alternator had been failing for awhile and the Cruise module was malfunctioning due to a low voltage condition?  <P>I think it was blind luck that the car made it home from our club get together a few weeks ago.<P>Now it is off to the Fiero Factory for the new clutch and other needed work.  <P>Thanks for the advice.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||12-19-2001||11:51 PM||||Glad you got it figured out, Bob.  I just had to put an alternator on my Formula about a month ago.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||12-20-2001||11:07 AM||||John:<P>We will be taking the maroon GT to Ed's for the clutch and will be picking up Megan's coupe after having the engine replaced.  She is real excited to finally be getting her car back (delays were my fault not Ed's).  I really wanted to get it running so we could get both cars moved during the same trip.<P>Have a great holiday!<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
